# Rank 1702 / CVE-2021-31162 (GHSA-fjcw-72x2-rmmv, recorded CWE-415):
# rust-lang/rust's in-place collection specialization. `Vec::from_iter(iter)`
# where `iter` is a chain rooted at a `vec.into_iter()` reuses the source
# buffer: library/alloc/src/vec/source_iter_marker.rs copies the pipeline's
# items forward over the same allocation the source iterator is draining and
# then hands `dst_buf`/`len`/`cap` to `Vec::from_raw_parts`. The teardown at
# issue ran, at the vulnerable revision,
#
#   // drop any remaining values at the tail of the source
#   src.drop_remaining();          // into_iter.rs:88-93
#   // but prevent drop of the allocation itself once IntoIter goes out of scope
#   src.forget_allocation();       // into_iter.rs:96-101
#
# where `drop_remaining` is `unsafe { ptr::drop_in_place(self.as_mut_slice()) }`
# followed by `self.ptr = self.end`, and `forget_allocation` is the field
# relinquish `self.cap = 0; self.buf = RawVec::NEW.ptr(); ...`. The order is
# the defect. `drop_in_place` runs arbitrary user `T::drop` while `src` still
# owns the allocation -- `cap` is nonzero and `ptr` has not been advanced past
# the remaining elements, because both of those writes sit *after* the
# destructor call. A `T::drop` that panics therefore unwinds with `src`
# unmodified, and `IntoIter`'s own destructor (into_iter.rs:231) re-enters on
# that same buffer: `DropGuard::drop` calls
# `RawVec::from_raw_parts_in(self.0.buf.as_ptr(), self.0.cap, alloc)`, freeing
# the allocation that backs the `Vec::from_raw_parts(dst_buf, len, cap)`
# being constructed, and `ptr::drop_in_place(guard.0.as_raw_mut_slice())`
# drops the remaining elements a second time. Double free and destructor
# re-entry from one ordering.
#
# The fixed revision (836fef0, cherry-picked from 421f5d28) merges the two helpers into
# `forget_allocation_drop_remaining`, which saves the raw slice, resets
# `cap`/`buf`/`ptr`/`end` to an empty zero-capacity state *first*, and only
# then runs `ptr::drop_in_place(remaining)`. A panicking drop now finds the
# iterator relinquished, so its destructor drops nothing and frees
# nothing; the documented trade-off is that items collected leak.
# The same discipline is applied correctly elsewhere in the file -- mod.rs:1449
# reads `// Advance early to avoid double drop if drop_in_place panicked.` --
# which is what marks this as an ordering bug rather than a missing check.
#
# Pool row note: f97769a2 is the bors merge of the beta backport PR #84603 and
# backports two unrelated advisories; the readable sibling for this CVE is
# 836fef0, whose parent 6ed66eb is the vulnerable state. The merge's other
# parent cc6d66f is equally vulnerable. .
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, three absences stack, and the one binding that
# discriminates is the fixing commit's own layout.
#
# Nothing is labelled at the defect site. `vyql match` on
# source_iter_marker.rs reports only code.UnsafeMutableAlias on the pointer
# *read* block at :28 (VYQL-MEM-009, firing identically on both revisions);
# into_iter.rs, where the change lands, reports nothing at all. Scanning the
# extracted library/alloc/src/vec subtree gives 13 findings on the vulnerable
# revision and 13 on the fixed one, every one of them a LOW VYQL-MEM-006 on
# mod.rs or set_len_on_drop.rs -- findings in the neighbourhood, none on the
# drop sequence.
#
# No label or pair of labels discriminates, because the reachable shape is
# identical on both sides: 8 `drop_in_place` calls, 1 `self.cap = 0` site, 1
# `RawVec::from_raw_parts_in` site, 1 `Vec::from_raw_parts` site. The fixed revision moves
# a `drop_in_place` from one function into another and reverses its order
# against a field write; it removes nothing and adds no API call. The only
# ordering fact that distinguishes the revisions is spread across two functions
# joined at a call site -- and `vyql resolve` on source_iter_marker.rs lists
# both `src.drop_remaining` (:81) and `src.forget_allocation` (:83) as
# UNRESOLVED, so no relation can cross them. That is the Rust delegated-call
# resolution gap recorded at rank 1364, not a new one.
#
# The candidate that does discriminate is built and measured, and rejected:
# a function-scoped presence binding on the relinquish helper
# (`assign contains "self.cap=0" and not call contains "drop_in_place"`)
# emitting the existing CWE-415 concept code.ClosureUnwindStaleLength moves
# this tree 13 -> 14 on the vulnerable revision and 13 -> 13 on the fixed one.
# It is also measured against an equally-correct alternative fix that simply
# swaps the two calls at the call site and keeps both helpers separate -- and
# it still reports 14. It keys on the fact that the fixed revision *merged two functions
# into one body*, not on the weakness: `self.cap = 0` is a `pub(super)` field
# write inside std, present in no library documentation, so the genericity test
# fails outright. Pinned below as case (c) so that binding cannot land silently.
#
# Closing this properly needs three things the engine does not have:
# resolution of delegated Rust method calls (1364), a destructor-invocation
# label for `ptr::drop_in_place` (Rust has no code.PointerFree or
# code.PointerUse binding at all, so VYQL-MEM-008 and VYQL-MEM-007 can never
# fire in Rust), and -- decisively -- a panic-unwind relation expressing "if
# this call panics, the following statements do not run and the owner's
# destructor re-enters." That last is a new control-flow/dataflow relation, not
# a localized correction, so this is not a mechanical fix. It is the Rust
# instance of the native memory-lifetime family (1509, 1511, 1516, 1527, 1528,
# 1530, 1543, 1547, 1561, 1576, 1590).
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1702: (a) the in-place collect specialization drops the source tail before relinquishing the allocation it shares with the destination"
  lang rust
  file src/vec/source_iter_marker.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  reject VYQL-MEM-052
  code
  ```
  use core::mem::ManuallyDrop;
  use core::ptr::{self, NonNull};

  pub struct RawVec<T> {
      ptr: NonNull<T>,
      cap: usize,
  }

  impl<T> RawVec<T> {
      pub const NEW: Self = Self { ptr: unsafe { NonNull::new_unchecked(1 as *mut T) }, cap: 0 };
  }

  pub struct IntoIter<T> {
      pub buf: NonNull<T>,
      pub cap: usize,
      pub ptr: *const T,
      pub end: *const T,
  }

  impl<T> IntoIter<T> {
      fn len(&self) -> usize {
          self.end as usize - self.ptr as usize
      }

      fn as_mut_slice(&mut self) -> &mut [T] {
          unsafe { &mut *ptr::slice_from_raw_parts_mut(self.ptr as *mut T, self.len()) }
      }

      fn as_raw_mut_slice(&mut self) -> *mut [T] {
          ptr::slice_from_raw_parts_mut(self.ptr as *mut T, self.len())
      }

      // the vulnerable form: destructor code runs on the tail while `self`
      // still owns the allocation -- `self.ptr = self.end` sits *after* the
      // drop, and `forget_allocation` is a separate call that never happens
      // if a user Drop panics here.
      pub(super) fn drop_remaining(&mut self) {
          unsafe {
              ptr::drop_in_place(self.as_mut_slice());
          }
          self.ptr = self.end;
      }

      // relinquishes the backing allocation, but only if reached
      pub(super) fn forget_allocation(&mut self) {
          self.cap = 0;
          self.buf = unsafe { NonNull::new_unchecked(RawVec::NEW.ptr.as_ptr()) };
          self.ptr = self.buf.as_ptr();
          self.end = self.buf.as_ptr();
      }
  }

  impl<T> Drop for IntoIter<T> {
      fn drop(&mut self) {
          struct DropGuard<'a, T>(&'a mut IntoIter<T>);

          impl<T> Drop for DropGuard<'_, T> {
              fn drop(&mut self) {
                  // RawVec handles deallocation
                  let _ = RawVec::from_raw_parts_in(self.0.buf.as_ptr(), self.0.cap);
              }
          }

          let guard = DropGuard(self);
          // destroy the remaining elements -- a second time, on unwind
          unsafe {
              ptr::drop_in_place(guard.0.as_raw_mut_slice());
          }
      }
  }

  // the ordering that is the defect: the tail is destroyed while the source
  // still holds the allocation the destination was built in
  fn from_iter_in_place<T>(mut src: IntoIter<T>) -> Vec<T> {
      let dst_buf = src.buf.as_ptr() as *mut T;
      let dst_end = src.end as *const T;
      let cap = src.cap;

      let mut dst = dst_buf;
      // ... pipeline writes items forward through dst ...

      // drop any remaining values at the tail of the source
      src.drop_remaining();
      // but prevent drop of the allocation itself once IntoIter goes out of scope
      src.forget_allocation();

      let len = unsafe { dst.offset_from(dst_buf) as usize };
      let vec = unsafe { Vec::from_raw_parts(dst_buf, len, cap) };
      let _ = ManuallyDrop::new(dst_end);
      vec
  }
  ```

test "rank1702: (b) the fixed revision relinquishes the allocation first and drops the tail through a raw slice saved beforehand"
  lang rust
  file src/vec/source_iter_marker.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  reject VYQL-MEM-052
  code
  ```
  use core::ptr::{self, NonNull};

  pub struct RawVec<T> {
      ptr: NonNull<T>,
      cap: usize,
  }

  impl<T> RawVec<T> {
      pub const NEW: Self = Self { ptr: unsafe { NonNull::new_unchecked(1 as *mut T) }, cap: 0 };
  }

  pub struct IntoIter<T> {
      pub buf: NonNull<T>,
      pub cap: usize,
      pub ptr: *const T,
      pub end: *const T,
  }

  impl<T> IntoIter<T> {
      fn len(&self) -> usize {
          self.end as usize - self.ptr as usize
      }

      fn as_raw_mut_slice(&mut self) -> *mut [T] {
          ptr::slice_from_raw_parts_mut(self.ptr as *mut T, self.len())
      }

      // the patched form: the owner is empty and zero-capacity *before* any
      // destructor runs, so a panicking drop finds nothing to re-drop and
      // nothing to free.
      pub(super) fn forget_allocation_drop_remaining(&mut self) {
          let remaining = self.as_raw_mut_slice();

          self.cap = 0;
          self.buf = unsafe { NonNull::new_unchecked(RawVec::NEW.ptr.as_ptr()) };
          self.ptr = self.buf.as_ptr();
          self.end = self.buf.as_ptr();

          unsafe {
              ptr::drop_in_place(remaining);
          }
      }
  }

  impl<T> Drop for IntoIter<T> {
      fn drop(&mut self) {
          struct DropGuard<'a, T>(&'a mut IntoIter<T>);

          impl<T> Drop for DropGuard<'_, T> {
              fn drop(&mut self) {
                  let _ = RawVec::from_raw_parts_in(self.0.buf.as_ptr(), self.0.cap);
              }
          }

          let guard = DropGuard(self);
          unsafe {
              ptr::drop_in_place(guard.0.as_raw_mut_slice());
          }
      }
  }

  fn from_iter_in_place<T>(mut src: IntoIter<T>) -> Vec<T> {
      let dst_buf = src.buf.as_ptr() as *mut T;
      let mut dst = dst_buf;
      let cap = src.cap;

      // if the drop panics then we also leak any elements collected into dst_buf
      src.forget_allocation_drop_remaining();

      let len = unsafe { dst.offset_from(dst_buf) as usize };
      unsafe { Vec::from_raw_parts(dst_buf, len, cap) }
  }
  ```

test "rank1702: (c) the same two helpers called in the safe order are an equally correct fix, and a binding keying on the helpers' co-location would flag it"
  lang rust
  file src/vec/source_iter_marker.rs
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  reject VYQL-MEM-052
  code
  ```
  use core::ptr::{self, NonNull};

  pub struct RawVec<T> {
      ptr: NonNull<T>,
      cap: usize,
  }

  impl<T> RawVec<T> {
      pub const NEW: Self = Self { ptr: unsafe { NonNull::new_unchecked(1 as *mut T) }, cap: 0 };
  }

  pub struct IntoIter<T> {
      pub buf: NonNull<T>,
      pub cap: usize,
      pub ptr: *const T,
      pub end: *const T,
  }

  impl<T> IntoIter<T> {
      fn len(&self) -> usize {
          self.end as usize - self.ptr as usize
      }

      fn as_mut_slice(&mut self) -> &mut [T] {
          unsafe { &mut *ptr::slice_from_raw_parts_mut(self.ptr as *mut T, self.len()) }
      }

      fn as_raw_mut_slice(&mut self) -> *mut [T] {
          ptr::slice_from_raw_parts_mut(self.ptr as *mut T, self.len())
      }

      pub(super) fn drop_remaining(&mut self) {
          unsafe {
              ptr::drop_in_place(self.as_mut_slice());
          }
          self.ptr = self.end;
      }

      pub(super) fn forget_allocation(&mut self) {
          self.cap = 0;
          self.buf = unsafe { NonNull::new_unchecked(RawVec::NEW.ptr.as_ptr()) };
          self.ptr = self.buf.as_ptr();
          self.end = self.buf.as_ptr();
      }
  }

  impl<T> Drop for IntoIter<T> {
      fn drop(&mut self) {
          struct DropGuard<'a, T>(&'a mut IntoIter<T>);

          impl<T> Drop for DropGuard<'_, T> {
              fn drop(&mut self) {
                  let _ = RawVec::from_raw_parts_in(self.0.buf.as_ptr(), self.0.cap);
              }
          }

          let guard = DropGuard(self);
          unsafe {
              ptr::drop_in_place(guard.0.as_raw_mut_slice());
          }
      }
  }

  // the helpers are left separate, but the relinquish now runs first: a
  // panicking drop finds an empty, zero-capacity iterator. This is a correct
  // remediation, and a function-scoped binding that fires on
  // `self.cap = 0` without a `drop_in_place` in the same body reports on it.
  fn from_iter_in_place<T>(mut src: IntoIter<T>) -> Vec<T> {
      let dst_buf = src.buf.as_ptr() as *mut T;
      let mut dst = dst_buf;
      let cap = src.cap;

      src.forget_allocation();
      src.drop_remaining();

      let len = unsafe { dst.offset_from(dst_buf) as usize };
      unsafe { Vec::from_raw_parts(dst_buf, len, cap) }
  }
  ```
